// Rep Max questions: Computer Science (OCR H446). One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from data/spec.js), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 one-rep max),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// A line that isn't valid is skipped and listed at the bottom of the home screen. Lines starting // are ignored.
{"id":"cs-001","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"At the very start of the fetch stage of the fetch-decode-execute cycle, which transfer takes place?","o":["The contents of the MAR are copied to the PC","The instruction in the CIR is copied to the MDR","The contents of the PC are copied to the MAR","The contents of the ACC are copied to the MAR"],"a":2,"x":"Fetch begins by copying the address in the PC into the MAR. The memory contents at that address then arrive in the MDR, the PC is incremented, and the MDR is copied to the CIR."}
{"id":"cs-002","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes Harvard architecture from Von Neumann architecture?","o":["Von Neumann has separate buses so an instruction and data can be fetched together","Harvard has separate memories and buses for instructions and for data","Harvard stores instructions and data together in one shared memory","Von Neumann has no registers, while Harvard has many"],"a":1,"x":"Harvard separates instruction and data memory (and buses), so both can be accessed at once. Von Neumann uses one shared memory and bus for both, which can cause a bottleneck."}
{"id":"cs-003","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A processor has a 3-stage pipeline (fetch, decode, execute), each stage taking one clock cycle. With no branches, how many cycles are needed to finish 5 instructions?","o":["5","7","8","15"],"a":1,"x":"The first instruction takes 3 cycles. After that, one instruction finishes every cycle, so 3 + 4 = 7. Without pipelining it would take 5 × 3 = 15."}
{"id":"cs-004","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":2,"q":"The PC holds 12 and the ACC holds 6. Address 12 contains ADD 40 (direct addressing) and address 40 holds the value 9. What are the PC and ACC after one complete fetch-decode-execute cycle?","o":["PC = 13, ACC = 46","PC = 13, ACC = 15","PC = 12, ACC = 15","PC = 13, ACC = 9"],"a":1,"x":"The PC is incremented during fetch (12 to 13). ADD 40 adds the value stored at address 40 (9), not the number 40, to the ACC: 6 + 9 = 15."}
{"id":"cs-005","s":"cs","t":"1.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which feature is typical of a RISC processor compared with a CISC processor?","o":["A large set of complex instructions of varying length","Fewer registers, relying on instructions that work on memory directly","More complex hardware needed to decode each instruction","A small set of simple instructions, most completing in one clock cycle"],"a":3,"x":"RISC uses a reduced set of simple, fixed-length instructions, often one cycle each, with more registers. CISC has many complex instructions that can take several cycles."}
{"id":"cs-006","s":"cs","t":"1.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A task is split across the 4 cores of a processor but only runs about 2.5 times faster than on one core. What is the most likely reason?","o":["Each core runs at one quarter of the clock speed","Some parts must run in sequence, and cores wait for each other's results","The cores cannot share the same RAM","Parallel processing only works on a GPU"],"a":1,"x":"Parts of a program that depend on earlier results cannot run in parallel, and cores need time to share data. So the speed-up is less than the number of cores."}
{"id":"cs-007","s":"cs","t":"1.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about SSDs compared with magnetic hard disks is correct?","o":["They are cheaper per gigabyte","They have no moving parts, giving faster access and better shock resistance","They store data as pits and lands read by a laser","They use a read/write head moving over spinning platters"],"a":1,"x":"SSDs use flash memory with no moving parts, so they are quicker to access and tougher if dropped. Magnetic disks are usually cheaper per GB."}
{"id":"cs-008","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A laptop with 4 GB of RAM is asked to run programs needing 5 GB in total. What does the operating system do to cope?","o":["Moves the extra programs into ROM temporarily","Moves inactive pages from RAM to a swap area on secondary storage","Permanently compresses the programs so they fit in RAM","Pauses the CPU until enough RAM is free"],"a":1,"x":"This is virtual memory: pages not currently needed are swapped out to secondary storage and swapped back in when required. Secondary storage is much slower than RAM, so heavy swapping causes thrashing."}
{"id":"cs-009","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly contrasts segmentation with paging?","o":["Pages vary in size to suit each program; segments are all the same size","Segmentation only works on secondary storage; paging only works in RAM","Paging divides memory by logical meaning; segmentation does not","Segments are variable-sized logical units; pages are fixed-size blocks"],"a":3,"x":"Paging splits memory into equal, fixed-size pages with no regard to content. Segmentation splits it into variable-sized logical units, such as a procedure or a data table."}
{"id":"cs-010","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"At the end of a fetch-decode-execute cycle, the processor finds an interrupt with a higher priority than the current task. What does it do first?","o":["Restores the saved registers from the stack","Loads the start address of the interrupt service routine into the PC","Saves the current register contents, including the PC, onto a stack","Runs the interrupt service routine from its first instruction"],"a":2,"x":"The registers, including the PC, must be pushed onto a stack first, or the return address would be lost. Then the ISR's address is loaded into the PC and the ISR runs. Finally the registers are restored."}
{"id":"cs-011","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Round robin scheduling uses a 3 ms time slice. A needs 4 ms, B needs 2 ms and C needs 6 ms, all ready at time 0 in the order A, B, C. When does A finish?","o":["9 ms","4 ms","6 ms","12 ms"],"a":0,"x":"A runs 0-3 ms and joins the back of the queue, behind B and C. B runs 3-5 ms and finishes. C runs 5-8 ms and rejoins behind A. A runs 8-9 ms and finishes at 9 ms; C ends at 12 ms."}
{"id":"cs-012","s":"cs","t":"1.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which stage of compilation removes comments and white space and breaks the source code into tokens?","o":["Lexical analysis","Syntax analysis","Code generation","Optimisation"],"a":0,"x":"Lexical analysis turns source code into tokens such as keywords, identifiers and operators. Syntax analysis then checks the tokens follow the grammar of the language."}
{"id":"cs-013","s":"cs","t":"1.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which is an advantage of dynamic linking (using a DLL) rather than static linking?","o":["The executable runs even if the library is missing","The executable file is larger and self-contained","No linker or loader is needed at all","Several programs can share one copy of the library in memory"],"a":3,"x":"With dynamic linking the library stays separate and is loaded when needed, so programs share it and it can be updated without recompiling. Static linking copies the library into every executable."}
{"id":"cs-014","s":"cs","t":"1.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which development methodology repeats a cycle in which risks are identified and analysed on every loop?","o":["Waterfall model","Rapid application development","Spiral model","Extreme programming"],"a":2,"x":"The spiral model goes round planning, risk analysis, development and evaluation, with risk analysis driving each loop. It suits large, high-risk projects."}
{"id":"cs-015","s":"cs","t":"1.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a feature of extreme programming?","o":["Pair programming, with two developers sharing one computer","A complete requirements document signed off before any coding","Risk analysis at the start of every spiral","Testing carried out only after all coding is complete"],"a":0,"x":"Extreme programming uses very short iterations, pair programming, test-first development and constant customer involvement. The other options describe waterfall or spiral."}
{"id":"cs-016","s":"cs","t":"1.2.4","lv":3,"q":"The Little Man Computer program shown is run. What does it do?","code":"       INP\n       STA a\n       INP\n       STA b\n       SUB a\n       BRP bigger\n       LDA a\n       OUT\n       HLT\nbigger LDA b\n       OUT\n       HLT\na      DAT\nb      DAT","o":["Outputs the smaller of the two numbers entered","Outputs the larger of the two numbers entered","Outputs the difference between the two numbers","Outputs the sum of the two numbers"],"a":1,"x":"It stores the inputs as a and b, then computes b - a. If that is not negative (BRP), b is at least as large, so b is output. Otherwise a is output. Try 4 then 9, and 9 then 4: both give 9."}
{"id":"cs-017","s":"cs","t":"1.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Location 30 holds 50 and location 50 holds 7. LDA 30 is executed using indirect addressing. What is loaded into the ACC?","o":["7","50","30","57"],"a":0,"x":"Indirect addressing treats the operand as the address of an address. Location 30 holds 50, so go to location 50 and load the value there: 7."}
{"id":"cs-018","s":"cs","t":"1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"class Pet\n    private sound\n    public procedure new()\n        sound = \"...\"\n    endprocedure\n    public function speak()\n        return sound\n    endfunction\nendclass\nclass Dog inherits Pet\n    public procedure new()\n        super.new()\n    endprocedure\n    public function speak()\n        return \"Woof\" + super.speak()\n    endfunction\nendclass\nd = new Dog()\nprint(d.speak())","o":["...","Woof","...Woof","Woof..."],"a":3,"x":"d is a Dog, so Dog's speak overrides Pet's. It returns \"Woof\" joined to super.speak(), Pet's speak, which returns sound. sound was set to \"...\" when Dog's constructor called super.new(). Result: Woof..."}
{"id":"cs-019","s":"cs","t":"1.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about assembly language is correct?","o":["It runs unchanged on any processor","Each instruction usually translates to one machine code instruction","It is translated to machine code by an interpreter","Programs are written entirely as strings of 1s and 0s"],"a":1,"x":"Assembly uses mnemonics such as ADD and LDA, with an assembler translating roughly one-to-one into machine code. It is specific to a processor family, so it is not portable."}
{"id":"cs-020","s":"cs","t":"1.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Run length encoding stores each run as an 8-bit count followed by an 8-bit character code. How many bits does it need to store AAAABBBCCDAA?","o":["64","96","80","40"],"a":2,"x":"The runs are AAAA, BBB, CC, D and AA: 5 runs, because the two A runs are not next to each other. 5 runs × 16 bits = 80 bits, against 96 bits uncompressed (12 characters × 8)."}
{"id":"cs-021","s":"cs","t":"1.3.1","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this Caesar cipher program runs?","code":"msg = \"BYTE\"\nkey = 5\nout = \"\"\nfor i = 0 to msg.length - 1\n    n = ASC(msg.substring(i, 1)) - 65\n    out = out + CHR((n + key) MOD 26 + 65)\nnext i\nprint(out)","o":["G^YJ","GDYJ","FCXI","HEZK"],"a":1,"x":"Each letter shifts 5 places, wrapping round the alphabet: B to G, Y to D, T to Y, E to J. Without the MOD 26, Y would become the symbol ^."}
{"id":"cs-022","s":"cs","t":"1.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Why do websites store a hash of each password rather than an encrypted copy?","o":["A hash cannot be reversed, so a stolen database does not reveal passwords","A hash can be decrypted with a key if the user forgets their password","Hashing produces a longer output, which is harder to guess","Hashing is reversible, so passwords can be emailed to users"],"a":0,"x":"Hashing is one-way: the site hashes what you type at login and compares it with the stored hash. Encrypted data could be decrypted if the key were stolen."}
{"id":"cs-023","s":"cs","t":"1.3.2","lv":1,"q":"In a database, Orders.CustomerID is a foreign key referencing Customers. What does referential integrity prevent?","o":["Adding an order for a CustomerID that does not exist in Customers","Two customers sharing the same name","An order having more than one product","A customer having more than one order"],"a":0,"x":"Referential integrity means every foreign key value must match an existing primary key in the linked table. It stops orphan records such as an order for a non-existent customer."}
{"id":"cs-024","s":"cs","t":"1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Table Pupil(PupilID, Name, Form, Score). Which query lists only the names of pupils in form 12B who scored over 60, highest score first?","o":["SELECT Name FROM Pupil WHERE Form = '12B' OR Score > 60 ORDER BY Score DESC","SELECT Name FROM Pupil WHERE Form = '12B' AND Score > 60 ORDER BY Score","SELECT * FROM Pupil WHERE Form = '12B' AND Score > 60 ORDER BY Score DESC","SELECT Name FROM Pupil WHERE Form = '12B' AND Score > 60 ORDER BY Score DESC"],"a":3,"x":"Both conditions are needed, so use AND. DESC puts the highest first (the default is ascending). SELECT Name returns only names, whereas SELECT * returns every field."}
{"id":"cs-025","s":"cs","t":"1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"OrderLine(OrderID, ProductID, ProductName, Quantity) has the composite key (OrderID, ProductID). ProductName depends only on ProductID. Which normal form does it fail first?","o":["Second normal form (2NF)","First normal form (1NF)","Third normal form (3NF)","None: it is already in 3NF"],"a":0,"x":"All values are atomic, so 1NF is met. ProductName depends on only part of the composite key (a partial dependency), which breaks 2NF. Fix it by moving ProductName into a Product table."}
{"id":"cs-026","s":"cs","t":"1.3.2","lv":1,"q":"A bank transfer takes £50 from account A, but the system crashes before crediting account B. The database undoes the first step. Which ACID property is this?","o":["Consistency","Isolation","Durability","Atomicity"],"a":3,"x":"Atomicity means a transaction happens completely or not at all. Rolling back the partial transfer stops money vanishing."}
{"id":"cs-027","s":"cs","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Which layer of the TCP/IP stack adds the source and destination IP addresses to each packet?","o":["Application layer","Transport layer","Internet (network) layer","Link layer"],"a":2,"x":"The Internet layer handles IP addressing and routing. The Transport layer (TCP) adds port numbers and sequence numbers, and the Link layer deals with MAC addresses on the local network."}
{"id":"cs-028","s":"cs","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about packet switching is correct?","o":["A dedicated path is reserved for the whole communication before sending","Packets may take different routes and are reassembled using sequence numbers","If packets arrive out of order the whole message is lost","Each packet carries a full copy of the message in case of loss"],"a":1,"x":"Packets are routed independently, so they can arrive out of order. The destination puts them back together using sequence numbers. Reserving a dedicated path is circuit switching."}
{"id":"cs-029","s":"cs","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"What does a DNS server do when your browser asks about www.example.com?","o":["Returns the IP address that belongs to that domain name","Returns the web page for that domain","Encrypts the request before it is sent","Assigns your device a new IP address"],"a":0,"x":"DNS translates human-friendly domain names into IP addresses. The browser then uses the IP address to contact the web server. Assigning addresses to devices is DHCP."}
{"id":"cs-030","s":"cs","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"What does a packet-filtering firewall do?","o":["Scans every file on the computer for known viruses","Encrypts all outgoing packets so they cannot be read","Checks each packet's addresses and ports against rules and allows or blocks it","Repeats lost packets until they are delivered"],"a":2,"x":"A firewall inspects packet headers (source and destination IP, port, protocol) against its rules. Virus scanning is done by anti-malware software."}
{"id":"cs-031","s":"cs","t":"1.3.4","lv":2,"q":"Which action must be done server-side rather than by JavaScript running in the browser?","o":["Checking a submitted password against the hashed copy in the database","Checking that an email field contains an @ symbol","Showing or hiding a menu when a button is clicked","Changing a button's colour when the mouse hovers over it"],"a":0,"x":"Anything involving secret or stored data, such as the user database, must run on the server because client-side code can be seen and changed by the user. The other tasks are fine in the browser."}
{"id":"cs-032","s":"cs","t":"1.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which factor most increases a web page's PageRank?","o":["Repeating the search keyword many times on the page","Links to it from many other highly ranked pages","Being stored on a faster web server","Having a longer URL"],"a":1,"x":"PageRank counts links into a page, giving more weight to links from pages that are themselves highly ranked. Keyword stuffing and server speed are not part of the algorithm."}
{"id":"cs-033","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":1,"q":"What is the binary number 10110110 in hexadecimal?","o":["6B","A6","B9","B6"],"a":3,"x":"Split into nibbles: 1011 = 11 = B and 0110 = 6. So 10110110 = B6."}
{"id":"cs-034","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"What is -45 in 8-bit two's complement?","o":["11010011","10101101","11010010","00101101"],"a":0,"x":"45 = 00101101. Invert the bits: 11010010. Add 1: 11010011. 10101101 is sign and magnitude, and 11010010 is only the one's complement."}
{"id":"cs-035","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"Adding 01101100 and 01001110 as 8-bit two's complement numbers gives 10111010. Why is this result wrong?","o":["Underflow: the true sum is smaller than -128","A rounding error from converting to floating point","A parity error: the result has an odd number of 1s","Overflow: the true sum, 186, is bigger than the maximum of 127"],"a":3,"x":"108 + 78 = 186, but the largest 8-bit two's complement number is 127. The result spills into the sign bit, so 10111010 wrongly reads as -70."}
{"id":"cs-036","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"An 8-bit value x is stored in a register. Which operation sets bits 0 and 7 to 1 and leaves every other bit unchanged?","o":["x AND 10000001","x XOR 10000001","x OR 01111110","x OR 10000001"],"a":3,"x":"OR with a 1 forces a bit to 1, and OR with a 0 leaves it alone. AND would clear the other bits, and XOR would toggle bits 0 and 7 rather than set them."}
{"id":"cs-037","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":3,"q":"A floating-point number has an 8-bit two's complement mantissa and a 4-bit two's complement exponent. Mantissa 00011010 and exponent 0101 are normalised. What is the new exponent?","o":["0011","0111","0100","0101"],"a":0,"x":"A positive normalised mantissa must start 01. Shift 00011010 left twice to get 01101000. Each left shift doubles the mantissa, so take 1 off the exponent per shift: 5 - 2 = 3 = 0011. The value stays 6.5."}
{"id":"cs-038","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":1,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"word = \"HAL\"\nout = \"\"\nfor i = 0 to word.length - 1\n    out = out + CHR(ASC(word.substring(i, 1)) + 1)\nnext i\nprint(out)","o":["GZK","IBM","HAL","JCN"],"a":1,"x":"ASC gives the code of each letter, 1 is added, and CHR converts back, so every letter moves one place on: H to I, A to B, L to M."}
{"id":"cs-039","s":"cs","t":"1.4.2","lv":1,"q":"A Stack class has methods push (adds an item to the top) and pop (removes and returns the top item). What is printed?","code":"s = new Stack()\ns.push(5)\ns.push(8)\ns.pop()\ns.push(3)\ns.push(6)\ns.pop()\nprint(s.pop())","o":["3","5","6","8"],"a":0,"x":"After the operations the stack goes [5], [5,8], [5], [5,3], [5,3,6], [5,3]. The final pop removes and returns the top item, 3."}
{"id":"cs-040","s":"cs","t":"1.4.2","lv":2,"q":"A circular queue is held in an array of size 5 (indices 0 to 4). The front pointer holds 3, the index of the first item, and the queue holds 4 items. At which index is the next item added?","o":["0","1","2","4"],"a":2,"x":"The items sit at indices 3, 4, 0 and 1, because the queue wraps round. The next free slot is (3 + 4) MOD 5 = 2."}
{"id":"cs-041","s":"cs","t":"1.4.2","lv":3,"q":"A hash table has indices 0 to 6 and uses key MOD 7 with linear probing (try the next slot, wrapping from 6 to 0). Keys 20, 34, 13, 9 and 27 are inserted in that order. Where is 27 stored?","o":["0","2","3","6"],"a":2,"x":"Home slots: 20, 34, 13 and 27 hash to 6; 9 hashes to 2. 20 takes 6, 34 wraps to 0, 13 goes to 1, 9 takes 2. 27 tries 6, 0, 1 and 2, all full, so it goes in 3."}
{"id":"cs-042","s":"cs","t":"1.4.2","lv":2,"q":"The values 8, 3, 10, 1, 6, 14, 4, 7 are inserted in that order into an empty binary search tree. What is the parent of 7?","o":["3","4","8","6"],"a":3,"x":"7 < 8 so go left to 3. 7 > 3 so go right to 6. 7 > 6 and 6 has no right child, so 7 becomes the right child of 6."}
{"id":"cs-043","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"According to De Morgan's law, NOT (A AND B) is equivalent to which expression?","o":["(NOT A) AND (NOT B)","NOT A AND B","(NOT A) OR (NOT B)","A OR B"],"a":2,"x":"De Morgan: negate each term and swap AND with OR. So NOT (A AND B) = (NOT A) OR (NOT B), and NOT (A OR B) = (NOT A) AND (NOT B)."}
{"id":"cs-044","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":3,"q":"Q is 1 only for (A, B, C) = 001, 011, 110 and 111. Which is the simplest expression for Q?","o":["(NOT A AND C) OR (B AND C)","(NOT A AND C) OR (A AND B)","(A AND B) OR C","B OR C"],"a":1,"x":"On a Karnaugh map, 001 and 011 group as NOT A AND C, and 110 and 111 group as A AND B. The other options give a 1 for an input that should be 0 (for example 101) or miss 110."}
{"id":"cs-045","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"A full adder has inputs A = 1, B = 0 and carry in = 1. What are the sum and carry out?","o":["Sum = 1, carry out = 0","Sum = 1, carry out = 1","Sum = 0, carry out = 1","Sum = 0, carry out = 0"],"a":2,"x":"1 + 0 + 1 = 2, which is 10 in binary, so the sum bit is 0 and the carry out is 1."}
{"id":"cs-046","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":2,"q":"A rising-edge D-type flip-flop receives a rising clock edge with D = 1, so Q becomes 1. D then changes to 0 before the next rising edge. What is Q?","o":["Q becomes 0 immediately","Q stays at 1 until the next rising clock edge","Q becomes 0 when the clock next falls","Q becomes undefined"],"a":1,"x":"A D-type flip-flop only samples D on the clock edge. Between edges it holds its stored value, which is why it is used as a one-bit memory."}
{"id":"cs-047","s":"cs","t":"1.5.1","lv":1,"q":"Which action is an offence under the Computer Misuse Act 1990?","o":["Sharing a purchased film file with strangers online","Keeping customers' personal data for longer than necessary","Guessing a classmate's password to read their files without permission","Recording a friend's phone call with their agreement"],"a":2,"x":"Unauthorised access to computer material is the first offence in the Act, even if no damage is done. Sharing films is a copyright issue and retaining data is a Data Protection issue."}
{"id":"cs-048","s":"cs","t":"1.5.1","lv":2,"q":"A gym still holds the names, addresses and bank details of members who left 12 years ago, for no reason. Which Data Protection Act 2018 principle does this break?","o":["Data must be processed securely","Data must be accurate and kept up to date","Data must be kept no longer than necessary","Data must only be used for the purpose it was collected"],"a":2,"x":"This is the storage limitation principle: personal data should only be kept as long as there is a lawful reason to hold it."}
{"id":"cs-049","s":"cs","t":"1.5.2","lv":2,"q":"A hiring algorithm trained on 20 years of a company's past decisions rejects most female applicants, because the company mainly hired men. Which issue is this?","o":["The digital divide","Censorship","Algorithmic bias","Net neutrality"],"a":2,"x":"Machine learning copies patterns in its training data, including unfair ones. Biased data leads to biased decisions, which raises ethical and legal concerns."}
{"id":"cs-050","s":"cs","t":"2.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A London Underground map shows stations and lines but ignores real distances and the streets above. Which computational thinking technique does this show?","o":["Abstraction","Decomposition","Backtracking","Concurrency"],"a":0,"x":"Abstraction removes unnecessary detail so that only what matters (the connections) remains."}
{"id":"cs-051","s":"cs","t":"2.1.2","lv":1,"q":"A function calculates the average of the numbers in an array. Which is a sensible precondition for it?","o":["The function must use a loop","The result must be a whole number","The array must contain at least one number","The average must not exceed 100"],"a":2,"x":"A precondition states what must be true before the subroutine runs. With an empty array the function would divide by zero."}
{"id":"cs-052","s":"cs","t":"2.1.3","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"function step(n)\n    if n MOD 2 == 0 then\n        return n DIV 2\n    endif\n    return 3 * n + 1\nendfunction\nprint(step(step(step(6))))","o":["10","5","16","3"],"a":1,"x":"step(6) = 3 (even, so halve). step(3) = 10 (odd, so 3n + 1). step(10) = 5 (even, so halve). The innermost call runs first, so the answer is 5."}
{"id":"cs-053","s":"cs","t":"2.1.4","lv":2,"q":"How many lines of output does this program produce?","code":"for i = 1 to 15\n    if i MOD 3 == 0 AND i MOD 5 == 0 then\n        print(\"FB\")\n    elseif i MOD 3 == 0 then\n        print(\"F\")\n    elseif i MOD 5 == 0 then\n        print(\"B\")\n    endif\nnext i","o":["8","7","5","3"],"a":1,"x":"3, 5, 6, 9, 10, 12 and 15 each print one line. 15 matches the first condition, so it prints only once. Adding 5 + 3 gives 8, but 15 was counted twice."}
{"id":"cs-054","s":"cs","t":"2.1.5","lv":2,"q":"Two processes each read a shared balance of 100, add 50, and write the result back. Both read before either writes. What is the final balance?","o":["200","100","250","150"],"a":3,"x":"Both processes calculate 100 + 50 = 150 and write 150, so one update is lost instead of reaching 200. This is a race condition, and it is why shared data needs locking."}
{"id":"cs-055","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"total = 0\nfor i = 1 to 10 step 3\n    total = total + i\nnext i\nprint(total)","o":["22","12","35","55"],"a":0,"x":"The loop variable takes the values 1, 4, 7, 10 (the end value is inclusive). 1 + 4 + 7 + 10 = 22."}
{"id":"cs-056","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":3,"q":"The function f is called as f(5). Including that first call, how many times is f called in total?","code":"function f(n)\n    if n <= 1 then\n        return 1\n    endif\n    return f(n - 1) + f(n - 2)\nendfunction\nprint(f(5))","o":["8","9","15","11"],"a":2,"x":"Calls for f(n) = 1 + calls(n-1) + calls(n-2). Starting from 1 call each for f(0) and f(1), the totals are 3, 5, 9 and 15 for n = 2, 3, 4, 5. f(5) returns 8, a common trap."}
{"id":"cs-057","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"procedure bump(a:byVal, b:byRef)\n    a = a + 10\n    b = b + 10\nendprocedure\n\nx = 1\ny = 1\nbump(x, y)\nprint(str(x) + \",\" + str(y))","o":["1,1","11,11","11,1","1,11"],"a":3,"x":"a is passed by value, so the procedure changes only a copy and x stays 1. b is passed by reference, so y itself becomes 11."}
{"id":"cs-058","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":3,"q":"What is printed when this program runs? Parameters are passed by value unless byRef is stated.","code":"global total = 0\n\nprocedure add(n)\n    total = total + n\n    n = 100\nendprocedure\n\nn = 5\nadd(n)\nadd(n)\nprint(total + n)","o":["205","110","15","10"],"a":2,"x":"total is global, so both calls add 5 to it: total = 10. The parameter n is passed by value, so setting it to 100 inside the procedure does not change the main n, which stays 5. 10 + 5 = 15."}
{"id":"cs-059","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Which statement about this program is correct?","code":"array scores[5]\nscores[0] = 7\nscores[1] = 3\nscores[2] = 9\nscores[3] = 4\nscores[4] = 6\ntotal = 0\nfor i = 0 to 5\n    total = total + scores[i]\nnext i","o":["The loop runs 5 times and total finishes as 29","The loop stops at i = 4, because 'to 5' excludes 5","The program is fine, but total is 35 because i is added too","The loop runs 6 times, so scores[5] is outside the array and causes an error"],"a":3,"x":"A for loop includes its end value, so i goes 0 to 5 (6 times). The array has indices 0 to 4, so scores[5] is out of range. The fix is for i = 0 to 4."}
{"id":"cs-060","s":"cs","t":"2.2.2","lv":3,"q":"The function power is called as power(2, 10). Including that first call, how many times is power called in total?","code":"function power(b, e)\n    if e == 0 then\n        return 1\n    endif\n    half = power(b, e DIV 2)\n    if e MOD 2 == 0 then\n        return half * half\n    endif\n    return half * half * b\nendfunction\nprint(power(2, 10))","o":["5","4","6","10"],"a":0,"x":"The exponent halves each time: 10, 5, 2, 1, 0. That is 5 calls. Halving the problem each time is divide and conquer."}
{"id":"cs-061","s":"cs","t":"2.2.2","lv":1,"q":"What is a heuristic?","o":["A method that always finds the best solution, however slowly","A way of splitting a problem into two halves","A rule of thumb that finds a good solution quickly, but not always the best one","A routine that undoes the last step when it hits a dead end"],"a":2,"x":"Heuristics trade perfect accuracy for speed, for example estimating the distance to the goal in A*. Undoing steps is backtracking, and halving problems is divide and conquer."}
{"id":"cs-062","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"What is the time complexity of this loop in terms of n?","code":"i = 1\nwhile i < n\n    i = i * 2\nendwhile","o":["O(log n)","O(n)","O(n log n)","O(2^n)"],"a":0,"x":"i doubles each time (1, 2, 4, 8, ...), so it reaches n after about log2(n) steps. For n = 1024 it runs just 10 times."}
{"id":"cs-063","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A bubble sort is used to sort 6 3 5 1 4 into ascending order. What does the list look like after the first complete pass?","o":["3 5 6 1 4","3 1 4 5 6","1 3 4 5 6","3 5 1 4 6"],"a":3,"x":"Compare neighbours and swap if needed: 6 bubbles all the way along, giving 3 5 1 4 6. The largest value is now in place. More passes are needed."}
{"id":"cs-064","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":3,"q":"How many lines of output does this program produce?","code":"low = 1\nhigh = 100\ndo\n    guess = (low + high) DIV 2\n    print(guess)\n    if guess < 90 then\n        low = guess + 1\n    else\n        high = guess - 1\n    endif\nuntil guess == 90","o":["5","6","8","7"],"a":3,"x":"The guesses printed are 50, 75, 88, 94, 91, 89 and 90. Each guess halves the remaining range (binary search), and the loop stops when guess is 90."}
{"id":"cs-065","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A binary tree has root 8. Node 3 is its left child, with children 1 and 6. Node 10 is its right child, with right child 14. Which is the post-order traversal?","o":["1, 6, 3, 14, 10, 8","8, 3, 1, 6, 10, 14","1, 3, 6, 8, 10, 14","8, 3, 10, 1, 6, 14"],"a":0,"x":"Post-order visits left subtree, right subtree, then the root. The other options are pre-order, in-order and breadth-first."}
{"id":"cs-066","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":3,"q":"An undirected graph has edges A-B 7, A-C 3, B-C 2, B-D 4, C-D 8, C-E 9, D-E 1. What is the shortest distance from A to E, as found by Dijkstra's algorithm?","o":["9","11","12","10"],"a":3,"x":"Dijkstra finalises A (0), C (3), B (5), D (9), then E (10). The route is A-C-B-D-E: 3 + 2 + 4 + 1 = 10. Direct routes such as A-C-E cost 12."}
{"id":"cs-067","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A* expands the open node with the lowest f = g + h. The open nodes are P (g=6, h=4), Q (g=3, h=9), R (g=5, h=3) and S (g=2, h=7). Which is expanded next?","o":["P","Q","S","R"],"a":3,"x":"f values: P = 10, Q = 12, R = 8, S = 9. R has the lowest, so it is expanded next. Dijkstra would use g alone and pick S."}
{"id":"cs-068","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"What is the main role of the control unit in the processor?","o":["It decodes each instruction and sends out control signals","It carries out arithmetic and logical operations on data","It holds the address of the next instruction to fetch","It stores the result of the most recent calculation"],"a":0,"x":"The control unit decodes each instruction and sends control signals to coordinate the ALU, registers, memory and buses. Arithmetic is the ALU's job, the next address is in the PC, and results are held in the ACC."}
{"id":"cs-069","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about the buses in a Von Neumann computer is correct?","o":["The data bus is one-way, carrying data only from memory to the processor","The address bus is one-way, carrying addresses from the processor to memory","The address bus carries the data values that are read from memory","The control bus carries only the data values being written to memory"],"a":1,"x":"Addresses are always sent out by the processor, so the address bus is one-way. The data bus is two-way (reads and writes). The control bus carries signals such as read, write and clock, not data."}
{"id":"cs-070","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Why does a larger cache usually make a processor perform better?","o":["It raises the clock speed, so each cycle takes less time","It adds extra cores, so more instructions can run in parallel","It holds more of the data in use, so slow RAM is read less often","It widens the data bus, so more bits move in each transfer"],"a":2,"x":"Cache is small, fast memory next to the processor. A bigger cache holds more of the data and instructions in use, so the processor waits for slower main memory less often. Clock speed, cores and bus width are separate factors."}
{"id":"cs-071","s":"cs","t":"1.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A pipelined processor fetches the instructions after a branch while the branch is still executing. The branch is taken. What happens to those instructions?","o":["They are executed anyway and their results are kept","They wait in the pipeline until the branch target has finished","They are copied into cache so they can be reused later","They are discarded, because the program jumps somewhere else"],"a":3,"x":"The early fetches assumed the program carried on in order. When the branch jumps elsewhere, they are the wrong instructions, so the pipeline is flushed and fetching restarts at the target. This is why branches slow pipelines."}
{"id":"cs-073","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"What is the purpose of a device driver?","o":["It speeds up the device by keeping a copy of its data in cache","It converts general OS commands into ones a particular device understands","It scans the device for malicious software before use","It decides how much RAM each connected device may use"],"a":1,"x":"Each device understands its own set of commands. The driver acts as a translator between the operating system and that specific hardware, so the OS does not need to know the details of every device."}
{"id":"cs-074","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which describes the role of the BIOS when a computer is switched on?","o":["It loads every installed application into RAM","It moves pages between RAM and the hard disk as memory fills","It tests the hardware, then starts loading the operating system","It decides which process the processor will run first"],"a":2,"x":"The BIOS is stored in non-volatile memory. At start-up it runs a power-on self test of the hardware and then starts the bootstrap loader, which loads the operating system. Paging and scheduling are done by the OS after that."}
{"id":"cs-075","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"A computer with very little RAM spends most of its time moving pages between RAM and disk, and little useful work gets done. What is this called?","o":["Deadlock","Fragmentation","Pre-emption","Thrashing"],"a":3,"x":"Thrashing happens when RAM is so short that pages are swapped in and out constantly. Deadlock is processes waiting for each other's resources. Fragmentation is wasted or scattered memory, not constant swapping."}
{"id":"cs-076","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Jobs of length 7, 3, 2 and 4 ms all arrive at time 0 in that order. With shortest job first and no pre-emption, what is the average waiting time?","o":["4 ms","7.25 ms","8 ms","16 ms"],"a":0,"x":"SJF runs 2, 3, 4, 7. Waiting times are 0, 2, 5 and 9, a total of 16, so the average is 16 / 4 = 4 ms. 7.25 is first come first served, 8 is average turnaround time, and 16 forgets to divide."}
{"id":"cs-077","s":"cs","t":"1.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Shortest remaining time (pre-emptive): P1 arrives at 0 needing 6 ms, P2 arrives at 2 needing 3 ms, P3 arrives at 3 needing 1 ms. At what time does P2 finish?","o":["5 ms","6 ms","9 ms","10 ms"],"a":1,"x":"P1 runs 0-2 (4 left). P2 (3) pre-empts P1 at 2. At 3, P3 (1) pre-empts P2 (2 left), running 3-4. P2 then resumes and finishes at 6. 5 forgets P3, 9 is first come first served, 10 is non-pre-emptive SJF."}
{"id":"cs-078","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":1,"q":"Why is Unicode used instead of ASCII to store text in many languages?","o":["It stores every character in fewer bits, saving space","It stores characters in a form that is faster to compare","It has far more code points, so it covers many languages and symbols","It can only encode letters, so the character set is simpler"],"a":2,"x":"Standard ASCII uses 7 bits and has only 128 characters, enough for English text. Unicode has many thousands of code points covering world languages and symbols. It generally needs more bits per character, not fewer."}
{"id":"cs-079","s":"cs","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"a = \"7\"\nb = \"5\"\nprint(a + b)\nprint(int(a) + int(b))","o":["12, then 12","12, then 75","75, then 75","75, then 12"],"a":3,"x":"a and b are strings, so + joins them: \"75\". int() casts each string to an integer first, so the second + is addition: 12. The trap is thinking + always adds."}
{"id":"cs-080","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"Which logic gate produces the carry output of a half adder?","o":["AND","XOR","OR","NAND"],"a":0,"x":"A carry is produced only when both inputs are 1, which is the AND of the two bits. The sum output is the XOR of the inputs."}
{"id":"cs-081","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"Which expression is equivalent to A OR (A AND B)?","o":["B","A","A AND B","A OR B"],"a":1,"x":"This is the absorption law. If A is 1 the whole expression is 1, and if A is 0 then A AND B is also 0. So the result always equals A, whatever B is."}
{"id":"cs-082","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":2,"q":"Q is 1 only for (A, B, C) = 000, 001, 100 and 101. Which is the simplest expression for Q?","o":["B","NOT A AND NOT B","NOT B","NOT B AND NOT C"],"a":2,"x":"On a Karnaugh map the four 1s form one group of four, in which B = 0 and A and C take every value. A and C drop out, leaving NOT B. NOT A AND NOT B covers only 000 and 001."}
{"id":"cs-083","s":"cs","t":"1.4.3","lv":2,"q":"A circuit outputs Q = (NOT (A AND B)) AND (A OR B). Which single gate behaves in the same way?","o":["XNOR","NAND","NOR","XOR"],"a":3,"x":"NOT (A AND B) is 0 only for 11, and A OR B is 0 only for 00. Q is 1 exactly when the inputs differ, which is XOR. XNOR is the opposite; NAND and NOR give 1 for 00."}
{"id":"cs-085","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"What is printed when this program runs? Positions start at 0, and substring(start, numberOfChars) takes numberOfChars characters from position start.","code":"word = \"COMPUTER\"\nprint(word.substring(2, 3))\nprint(word.length)","o":["OMP, then 8","MPU, then 8","PUT, then 8","MPU, then 7"],"a":1,"x":"Position 2 of COMPUTER is M, and 3 characters give MPU. length counts all the characters, which is 8, not the last position (7)."}
{"id":"cs-086","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs? Parameters are passed by value unless byRef is stated.","code":"procedure swap(a:byVal, b:byRef)\n    t = a\n    a = b\n    b = t\nendprocedure\n\nx = 3\ny = 8\nswap(x, y)\nprint(x * 10 + y)","o":["83","38","33","88"],"a":2,"x":"a is a copy of x, so changing a leaves x as 3. b is y itself. a = b makes a 8, then b = t sets y to 3. The result is 33. 83 wrongly treats both as byRef."}
{"id":"cs-087","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"What is printed when this program runs?","code":"n = 4821\ntotal = 0\nwhile n > 0\n    total = total + (n MOD 10)\n    n = n DIV 10\nendwhile\nprint(total)","o":["14","534","1","15"],"a":3,"x":"Each pass adds the last digit (n MOD 10), then removes it (n DIV 10): 1 + 2 + 8 + 4 = 15. The loop stops when n reaches 0, so every digit is included. 534 adds n DIV 10 instead."}
{"id":"cs-088","s":"cs","t":"2.2.1","lv":3,"q":"What is printed when this program runs? calls is a global variable that rev can change.","code":"global calls = 0\n\nfunction rev(s)\n    calls = calls + 1\n    if s.length <= 1 then\n        return s\n    endif\n    return rev(s.substring(1, s.length - 1)) + s.substring(0, 1)\nendfunction\n\nprint(rev(\"STACK\"))\nprint(calls)","o":["KCATS, 5","STACK, 5","KCATS, 4","KCATS, 6"],"a":0,"x":"Each call moves the first letter to the end, so STACK becomes KCATS. rev is called on STACK, TACK, ACK, CK and K: 5 calls. K has length 1, so it stops."}
{"id":"cs-089","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"A list of 500,000 unsorted names is searched once for a single name. Which method is best?","o":["Binary search, because it always needs fewer comparisons","Binary search, because it also works on unsorted lists","Linear search, because sorting first would cost more than one search","Linear search, because it has a time complexity of O(log n)"],"a":2,"x":"Binary search needs a sorted list, and sorting 500,000 items takes far longer than one linear pass. Binary search is faster only when the list is already sorted or is searched many times."}
{"id":"cs-090","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"How many times does the while loop body run when this program runs?","code":"array data[8]\ndata[0] = 3\ndata[1] = 8\ndata[2] = 12\ndata[3] = 17\ndata[4] = 21\ndata[5] = 25\ndata[6] = 30\ndata[7] = 34\ntarget = 34\nlow = 0\nhigh = 7\ncount = 0\nwhile low <= high\n    mid = (low + high) DIV 2\n    count = count + 1\n    if data[mid] == target then\n        low = high + 1\n    elseif data[mid] < target then\n        low = mid + 1\n    else\n        high = mid - 1\n    endif\nendwhile\nprint(count)","o":["3","7","8","4"],"a":3,"x":"The midpoints are index 3, 5, 6 and 7, so 34 is found on the 4th pass. A linear search would need 8 passes. The search area roughly halves each time."}
{"id":"cs-091","s":"cs","t":"2.3.1","lv":2,"q":"After this code runs, what are the contents of array a, in order from a[0] to a[4]?","code":"array a[5]\na[0] = 5\na[1] = 2\na[2] = 9\na[3] = 1\na[4] = 6\nfor i = 1 to 3\n    temp = a[i]\n    j = i - 1\n    placed = false\n    while j >= 0 AND placed == false\n        if a[j] > temp then\n            a[j + 1] = a[j]\n            j = j - 1\n        else\n            placed = true\n        endif\n    endwhile\n    a[j + 1] = temp\nnext i","o":["2, 5, 9, 1, 6","1, 2, 5, 9, 6","1, 2, 5, 6, 9","1, 5, 2, 9, 6"],"a":1,"x":"Insertion sort, but the for loop stops at i = 3, so a[4] is never inserted. After i = 2 the array is 2, 5, 9, 1, 6. At i = 3 the 1 moves to the front, giving 1, 2, 5, 9, 6."}
